// **`$N` comptime-value parameters** (ADR-0087): a procedure whose parameter is polymorphic over a
// compile-time-known *value*, the value-side mirror of `$T`. W5's sixth sub-wave, and — like `$T`'s first
// (ADR-0081) — deliberately the *surface* only: a `$N` procedure parses, formats, lowers as a template,
// and **its body type-checks when declared**. *Calling* one is refused (E0271) until the second half,
// which is exercised in `type-errors/068`.
//
// The load-bearing difference from `$T`, and why more is checkable here: a `$T` parameter's *type* is
// unknown until a call, so its body cannot be checked; a `$N: s64` parameter's type is fully known
// (`s64`), and only its *value* varies — so `N` is a genuine `s64` in the body and the body type-checks
// soundly at template time. `sized`'s `N + 1` and `scaled`'s `N * factor` below are ordinary `s64`
// arithmetic, checked here, where a `$T` body could not be.
//
// What is checked here:
//
//   * a single `$N`, used in the body as an ordinary `s64` (`sized`);
//   * a `$N` beside an **ordinary** value parameter, so a template may mix the two (`scaled`);
//   * two `$N`/`$M` comptime parameters in one signature (`area`), the shape a later sub-wave
//     instantiates per tuple of values.
//
// A `$N` procedure lowers to **no MIR** — it is a template, skipped exactly as a `#foreign` body or a
// `$T` template is — so none appears in a MIR snapshot, which is correct: there is no value for `N` until
// a call fixes it.
#import "Basic";

/// The canonical example: one `$N`, used as an ordinary `s64` in the body.
sized :: ($N: s64) -> s64 {
    return N + 1;
}

/// A `$N` beside an ordinary value parameter — a template may mix comptime and runtime parameters.
scaled :: ($N: s64, factor: s64) -> s64 {
    return N * factor;
}

/// Two comptime-value parameters in one signature — the shape a later sub-wave keys per value tuple.
area :: ($N: s64, $M: s64) -> s64 {
    return N * M;
}

// None of the above is *called*, so `main` does ordinary work: the file's point is that declaring these
// checks clean and their bodies type-check, which no earlier sub-wave allowed — `$N` did not even parse.
main :: () {
    n := 7;
    exit(n);
}
